  "author_guidance": "In the 24-round test these actors stopped being able to do anything (or never could): cleaner (refused: knock-over: The tray is upright: was False, needed True); waiter (refused: carry-tray: The tray is upright: was False, needed True, knock-over: The tray is upright: was False, needed True). Every actor role must be able to act regularly: make sure some actor's action brings what each one's action needs to where it is checked (for example, if a carrier must hold goods before delivering, give another actor an action that loads goods onto the carrier, or let the carrier pick them up itself). A process may renew raw materials at their source or bring new demand, but never supply what an action makes or move goods between places. These moves use up several things and make nothing, so what they use simply disappears: sip-coffee. If the move turns them into something (a packed box, a meal, a finished part), add one to the field that holds what it makes, and let the next step's checks use that field.",
  "authority_rules": [
    "Every move and process must keep the world's laws: a resource with a total law changes in total only through the moves listed in may_change_by and through processes whose process_id is listed there as 'process:<process_id>'; every other effect on it subtracts from one holder exactly what it adds to another. A pairing law's move hands over its 'gives' and its 'pays' in the same move, with a check that each giver has enough. A process the laws name as 'process:<id>' must have exactly that process_id. The engine refuses any step that breaks a law. When there are no laws, this rule says nothing.",
    "Use only action participants as effect targets.",
    "Go through the represented things and moves for consequences that follow physically at once (a spill wets what is under it, a loud change such as an engine starting is heard by people in the same place) and write each one the represented state can hold as a process with timing immediately.",
